================================================================================
OPTION B — OPEN-CORE / DUAL LICENCE  (NOT RECOMMENDED HERE)
================================================================================

Presented for completeness. ../../gtm/07-marketplace-readiness.md §1 recommends against it
for this codebase, primarily because the plugin system is a runtime DLL loader
(TelegramAutoDownload.csproj:43-65), which makes an enforceable licence boundary between
"open core" and "paid plugin" impractical — the community would simply publish a free
replacement plugin.

If chosen, the structure would be:

  (a) The core application is licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0
      (AGPL-3.0), NOT MIT. AGPL is the correct choice for open-core because it requires
      anyone who modifies the software and makes it available over a network to publish their
      source, which prevents a competitor from running a closed hosted fork. MIT provides no
      such protection and should not be used.

  (b) Commercial features (multi-user, audit log, retention enforcement, subject search) live
      in separately licensed modules under the Option A terms above, and are NOT covered by
      the AGPL grant.

  (c) A commercial licence is offered to customers who cannot accept AGPL obligations.

  (d) A Contributor Licence Agreement (CLA) is MANDATORY from day one, so the owner retains
      the right to dual-license. Without it, accepting a single outside contribution
      permanently removes the ability to relicense.

Note that Option B does NOT reduce any of the legal risks identified in ../06-compliance.md.
It arguably increases content-liability exposure by publishing the source of the
DMCA §1201-exposed URL-download orchestration (see ../06-compliance.md §4.2).

================================================================================
WHAT MUST NOT BE DONE
================================================================================

Do NOT simply add an MIT LICENSE file to match the current README. That would:
  - irrevocably grant every existing and future user the right to resell the Software and to
    remove any licence-key enforcement, destroying the commercial model; and
  - be extremely difficult to reverse, because MIT grants are perpetual and irrevocable for
    versions already distributed under them.

If the owner's genuine intent is a free and open project with no commercial model, MIT is a
fine choice — but that decision should be made deliberately, not inherited from a one-word
README line.
